    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d696">

                <pb facs="0858" n="748" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg748"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.003/545: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-03-09T19:00:00-05:00">March 9, 1931—7
                    p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>92. Legation’s 52, February 6 [<hi rendition="#italic">3</hi>], 6 p.m.,<anchor
                        n="54" xml:id="d696-f1"/> and 101, March 4, 5 p.m.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Department has not received Yunnanfu’s despatch No. 14, December 30, 1930,
                        to the Legation.<anchor n="55" xml:id="d696-f2"/> Please forward copy and in
                        your discretion telegraph summary of item on page 3, as Department does <pb
                            facs="0859" n="749" xml:id="pg749"/> not understand why the Consul at
                        Yunnanfu has entered into a discussion with the Chinese authorities of
                        Yunnan in regard to extraterritorial rights when no specific case appears to
                        have arisen which would necessitate such a discussion.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>If no specific case requiring such discussion had arisen, the Department
                        desires that you issue a circular instruction cautioning all American
                        consular officers in China not to enter into discussions with the Chinese
                        authorities in regard to extraterritorial rights unless specific cases arise
                        which necessitate such action. In this event, consular officers should
                        continue to be guided by the Department’s telegrams to the Legation, No. 49,
                        February 8, 1 p.m., 1930,<anchor n="56" xml:id="d696-f3"/> and No. 92, March
                        12, noon, 1930.<anchor n="57" xml:id="d696-f4"/></item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Carr</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
